<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Hand-trimmed fixture standing in for WindowsFirewall-All-2.2, the latest revision PowerStig
  ships of the Windows Firewall STIG (#110's map). Rule ids, field values and dscresourcemodule
  attributes are copied from the real processed data in the local PowerStig clone rather than
  invented, trimmed the way the Google-Chrome and MS-Edge fixtures are (short Description, no
  VulnDiscussion/RawString prose, no LegacyId).

  Firewall carries two rule types: Registry (38 rules, every one the plain Registry dscresource
  rather than RegistryPolicyFile) and Manual (1 rule, V-242009, produces nothing). No Document, no
  unparsed rule, no duplicate and no Ensure=Absent rule, so there is nothing here to assert about
  those.

  The ten rules kept are the shapes #110's survey called out. Firewall is the first product whose
  sub-rule halves write different keys rather than differing only in value: every .a writes under
  SOFTWARE\Policies\Microsoft\WindowsFirewall and its .b under
  SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\SharedAccess\Parameters\FirewallPolicy, sharing only the
  ValueName. V-241989 is the pair whose two keys still end in the same leaf (DomainProfile).
  V-241990 is one of the three whose leaves disagree, which the map had said could not happen -
  the service key spells the private profile StandardProfile, so the two leaves are two spellings
  of one profile rather than two profiles. What such a block should be called is open at #119.
  V-241992 is the high-severity pair, V-241994 the low-severity one that is an organisation value
  on both halves - two variables, not one - and V-242004 one of the three ids that is not a
  sub-rule at all.
-->
